@section fdproxy

Read an input whose every byte a parent process serves, by offset, over two
pipes it hands down. A sandbox that leaves FFmpeg no path to the file and no
socket then still lets it read its input: the parent decides every read, and
storage that stops answering holds the parent, which can always end the run,
rather than FFmpeg waiting in the kernel.

The parent speaks the fdproxy protocol, specified at
@url{https://github.com/kstenerud/ffmpeg-fdproxy}. It creates the two pipes,
hands FFmpeg one end of each, and names them with the options below. The URL is
exactly @code{fdproxy:}, with nothing after it. Reading only; each pipe
serves one session at a time.

Like @code{fd:}, the protocol works on duplicates of the two descriptors and
never closes or changes them: they stay the caller's. It refuses the same
descriptor twice, stdin, stdout or stderr (@code{EINVAL}), a descriptor that is
no pipe or is open the wrong way (@code{EBADF}), and a pipe an open session
uses (@code{EBUSY}).

A write to a pipe whose reader has gone raises @code{SIGPIPE}, which the
program must ignore, as @command{ffmpeg} and @command{ffprobe} do.

This protocol accepts the following options:

@table @option
@item read_fd
The descriptor the parent's messages arrive on. Required.

@item write_fd
The descriptor this side's messages go out on. Required.

@item cache_blocks
How many of the parent's blocks to keep, from 2 to 1024. Default 32.
@end table

Its default whitelist is itself, so a demuxer's nested open cannot name one of
the channel's descriptors with @code{pipe:} or @code{fd:}. A program that sets
its own whitelist should still name only this protocol:
@example
ffmpeg -protocol_whitelist fdproxy -read_fd 4 -write_fd 5 -i fdproxy: ...
@end example

